WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.159 --> 00:00:03.337
(TV clicking)
(upbeat tinny music)

00:00:03.337 --> 00:00:04.329
- [Voiceover] Nearly everyone remembers

00:00:04.329 --> 00:00:06.267
Mister Rogers' Neighborhood.

00:00:06.267 --> 00:00:07.480
♫ It's a beautiful day in this neighborhood ♫

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:08.658
But not everyone knows

00:00:08.658 --> 00:00:11.671
that this cardigan-clad
king of children's TV,

00:00:11.671 --> 00:00:14.138
Fred Rogers, was actually a pioneer

00:00:14.138 --> 00:00:17.130
who challenged racial
stereotypes in media.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.258
Enter Francois Clemmons.

00:00:19.258 --> 00:00:21.938
- Francois Scarborough Clemmons.

00:00:21.938 --> 00:00:23.394
- [Voiceover] And his
role as Officer Clemmons

00:00:23.394 --> 00:00:25.631
made him one of the first
recurring black characters

00:00:25.631 --> 00:00:27.742
on a children's TV show.

00:00:27.742 --> 00:00:31.242
(upbeat electronic music)

00:00:32.424 --> 00:00:35.973
- When I started, there were
two, three shows, period,

00:00:35.973 --> 00:00:39.804
on television that
employed a black character.

00:00:39.804 --> 00:00:40.781
- [Voiceover] Francois grew up

00:00:40.781 --> 00:00:42.072
during the Civil Rights movement,

00:00:42.072 --> 00:00:44.527
in times of great racial
tension in the US.

00:00:44.527 --> 00:00:46.773
- So when Fred asked me
to be a police officer,

00:00:46.773 --> 00:00:48.820
"Fred, are you sure?

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.783
"Do you know what policemen
represent in the community

00:00:51.783 --> 00:00:53.472
"where I was raised?"

00:00:53.472 --> 00:00:57.042
And then he started talking
about children needing helpers,

00:00:57.042 --> 00:01:00.710
and the positive influence
that I could have

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:02.119
for young children.

00:01:02.119 --> 00:01:05.425
My heart opened as I listened to him.

00:01:05.425 --> 00:01:06.711
- [Voiceover] He accepted
the role, not knowing

00:01:06.711 --> 00:01:09.815
he would end up playing
Officer Clemmons for 30 years.

00:01:09.815 --> 00:01:11.719
And one of his most memorable scenes is also

00:01:11.719 --> 00:01:13.303
one of his favorites.

00:01:13.303 --> 00:01:15.503
♫ There are many ways

00:01:15.503 --> 00:01:18.402
♫ To say I love you, there are ♫

00:01:18.402 --> 00:01:19.798
- [Francois] It's a very big deal

00:01:19.798 --> 00:01:23.580
for me to be putting my
feet in the water with Fred.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.155
- [Voiceover] During
a time of segregation,

00:01:25.155 --> 00:01:27.563
the symbolism wasn't lost on Francois.

00:01:27.563 --> 00:01:29.979
- To say that he didn't
know what he was doing,

00:01:29.979 --> 00:01:33.196
or that he accidentally
stumbled into integration,

00:01:33.196 --> 00:01:36.707
or talking about racism or sexism,

00:01:36.707 --> 00:01:39.235
that's not Mister Rogers.

00:01:39.235 --> 00:01:42.104
It was well planned and well thought out.

00:01:42.104 --> 00:01:44.323
And I think it was very impactful.

00:01:44.323 --> 00:01:47.588
♫ There are many ways

00:01:47.588 --> 00:01:50.995
♫ To say I love you ♫

00:01:50.995 --> 00:01:52.691
- [Voiceover] That
impact was felt by many,

00:01:52.691 --> 00:01:54.971
but for Francois, it was personal.

00:01:54.971 --> 00:01:56.659
- I was in the studio one day,

00:01:56.659 --> 00:02:00.691
that particular day, he was
filming the end of the show.

00:02:00.691 --> 00:02:02.499
And when he got to the part, he said,

00:02:02.499 --> 00:02:04.863
"You make every day a special day."

00:02:04.863 --> 00:02:08.823
- You know how, by just your being you.

00:02:08.823 --> 00:02:10.199
- And I swear, it was like,

00:02:10.199 --> 00:02:12.560
he was looking right into my eyes.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.831
And when the music stopped, I said,

00:02:14.831 --> 00:02:17.457
"Fred, were you talking to me?"

00:02:17.457 --> 00:02:20.978
And he said, yes, I have been
talking to you for years,

00:02:20.978 --> 00:02:22.670
but you heard me today.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:25.253
(piano music)

